---
/**
 * /real-estate-practice — NY real estate salesperson practice exam.
 *
 * SPR-0092. Design spec: `decisions/real-estate-practice-exam.md`.
 *
 * The page shell is deliberately static prose plus one island. Everything
 * interactive lives in `PracticeExam.tsx`; everything indexable lives in the
 * `<article data-pagefind-body>` below. That split is intentional — the 150+
 * question bank should not end up in the search index, and the provenance and
 * disclaimer copy should.
 *
 * `client:load` rather than `client:visible`: the exam IS the page. There is
 * nothing worth showing before it hydrates, and deferring would leave a
 * visitor staring at a loading state they scrolled to on purpose.
 */
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PracticeExam from '~/components/react/PracticeExam.tsx';
import ScrollControls from '~/components/react/ScrollControls.tsx';
import type { ExamBank } from '~/lib/exam/types.ts';

/* Read the review date straight out of the bank rather than repeating it here.
   `scripts/validate-exam-bank.ts` ages this same field and fails the build once
   it passes eighteen months, so a second copy in this file would be a date that
   could silently drift out of sync with the one being policed.

   Parsed at build time with readFileSync, not `import`, so the 325KB bank does
   not get inlined into this page's output for the sake of one string. */
const bankPath = new URL('../../public/data/ny-real-estate-questions.json', import.meta.url);
const { meta } = JSON.parse(readFileSync(bankPath, 'utf8')) as ExamBank;
const lastReviewed = meta.updated;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Real Estate Practice Exam"
  description="A free practice exam for the New York State Real Estate Salesperson Examination. 75 original questions drawn from the Department of State's 77-hour qualifying curriculum, scored instantly, with explanations and statutory citations."
  searchable={true}
>
  <article class="exam-page" data-pagefind-body>
    <p class="lead">
      A practice run at the <strong>New York State Real Estate Salesperson Examination</strong>.
      Seventy-five questions, drawn at random and weighted to match the Department of State's
      77-hour qualifying curriculum. Answer them in any order, submit once, and see every
      answer explained.
    </p>

    <p class="exam-page-caveat">
      These questions are original, written from public sources — they are not real exam items,
      and this page is not affiliated with or endorsed by the New York Department of State.
      Nothing is saved: leaving or reloading the page starts a fresh exam.
    </p>
  </article>

  <section class="exam-page-mount">
    <PracticeExam client:load />
  </section>

  {/* Scroll affordances for a page that runs to ~33,000px with an attempt
      loaded. `client:idle` rather than `client:load` — these are navigation
      conveniences, so they can wait for the exam itself to finish hydrating. */}
  <ScrollControls client:idle />

  <article class="exam-page" data-pagefind-body>
    <h2 id="what-the-state-says">What the state says</h2>

    <p>
      It is worth separating what the Department of State actually publishes from what the
      test-prep industry repeats. DOS confirms the exam is <strong>multiple choice</strong>, that
      candidates get <strong>one and a half hours</strong>, that it is <strong>based on the
      77-hour qualifying curriculum</strong>, and that results come back as
      <strong>pass or fail with no numerical score</strong>. A passing result stays valid for two
      years, and the license itself runs for two years.
    </p>

    <p>
      The two numbers everyone quotes — <strong>75 questions</strong> and a
      <strong>70% passing score</strong> — are not among them. Neither figure appears in any DOS
      publication. They are near-universal on prep sites, and they are probably right, but there
      is a plausible way the first one went wrong: the state's own application instructions still
      reference the pre-2008 <em>75-hour</em> qualifying course, and a question count is an easy
      thing to conflate with a course length.
    </p>

    <p>
      This exam is built to 75 questions and grades against 53 correct, because that is the
      convention and it makes for a useful drill. The percentage it reports is a study aid. The
      real exam will not give you one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="where-these-come-from">Where these come from</h2>

    <p>
      New York administers this exam itself. There is no PSI or Pearson VUE contract behind it,
      which means there is no candidate handbook and no released sample items — and every
      "sample question" circulating online is somebody's copyrighted product or a recalled item
      that was never anyone's to publish.
    </p>

    <p>
      So these were written from scratch, against public sources only: the
      <a href="https://dos.ny.gov/real-estate-salesperson-77-hour-curriculum-eff-12212022" rel="noopener">
        77-hour curriculum
      </a> effective 12/21/2022, Real Property Law Article 12-A, and 19 NYCRR Part 175. The
      curriculum is the closest thing to a published specification that exists: nineteen subjects,
      each with an hour allocation and a glossary of key terms the state wrote itself. Every
      question here carries the statute or regulation its rule comes from, so you can check the
      answer rather than take it on faith.
    </p>

    <p>
      The subject mix follows the state's hour weights rather than being uniformly random. Law of
      Agency carries eleven of the state's seventy-seven hours and contributes eleven of the
      seventy-five questions; Real Estate Mathematics carries one hour and contributes one
      question. That last one surprises people — math is drilled far harder by prep courses than
      its share of the curriculum suggests.
    </p>

    <h2 id="fine-print">Fine print</h2>

    <p>
      This is study material, not legal advice, and law moves. One live example of why that
      matters: the $500 credit that used to let a New York seller skip the Property Condition
      Disclosure Statement <strong>was eliminated effective March 20, 2024</strong>, and seven
      flood-disclosure questions were added to the form. A great deal of published prep content —
      and the official curriculum outline itself, which predates the amendment — is still wrong
      about this. Verify anything you plan to rely on against current law.
    </p>

    <p>
      Nothing on this page is collected or stored. There is no account, no cookie, no progress
      saved between visits, and no answer of yours leaves your browser. The question bank is a
      static file; the grading happens on your own machine.
    </p>

    <p class="exam-page-reviewed">
      Questions last reviewed against current law: <time datetime={lastReviewed}>{lastReviewed}</time>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global>
  /* Astro wraps an island in <astro-island>, an unknown element that defaults
     to display: inline. An inline parent is not a block container, so the
     island root's `margin-inline: auto` centring silently does nothing.
     Scoped to this page's mount so BaseLayout's SteamTransition island is
     untouched. */
  .exam-page-mount astro-island {
    display: contents;
  }
</style>

<style>
  .exam-page {
    max-width: 72ch;
  }

  .exam-page .lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-fg);
  }

  .exam-page-caveat {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-fg-muted);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-brass-oxidized);
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .exam-page h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .exam-page-reviewed {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-fg-subtle);
  }

  .exam-page-mount {
    margin-block: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>
